div.pdf", "any_repository_has_fulltext": true}, "primary_location": {"id": "doi:10.1017/bpp.2022.17", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1017/bpp.2022.17", "is_oa": true, "landing_page_url": "https://doi.org/10.1017/bpp.2022.17", "pdf_url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/F304264D0BC894DAFA1A86EEFE50293E/S2398063X22000173a.pdf/div-class-title-agentic-preferences-a-foundation-for-nudging-when-preferences-are-endogenous-div.pdf", "source": {"id": "https://openalex.org/S4210205184", "display_name": "Behavioural Public Policy", "issn_l": "2398-063X", "issn": ["2398-063X", "2398-0648"], "is_oa": false, "is_in_doaj": false, "is_core": true, "listed_in": ["anvur-scientific", "cwts-core", "dongbi-c", "jufo-1", "norway-1", "poland-20", "russia-white-list-4", "vabb-shw"], "host_organization": "https://openalex.org/P4310311721", "host_organization_name": "Cambridge University Press", "host_organization_lineage": ["https://openalex.org/P4310311721", "https://openalex.org/P4310311702"], "host_organization_lineage_names": ["Cambridge University Press", "University of Cambridge"], "type": "journal"}, "license": "cc-by", "license_id": "https://openalex.org/licenses/cc-by", "version": "publishedVersion", "is_accepted": true, "is_published": true, "raw_source_name": "Behavioural Public Policy", "raw_type": "journal-article"}, "abstract": "Abstract Since the publication of the seminal book Nudge by Thaler and Sunstein, several critics have highlighted preference endogeneity as a serious obstacle to nudging. When individuals hold preferences that are dynamic and endogenous to the nudge frame, it is unclear what the normative benchmark for libertarian paternalistic policies should be. While acknowledging this issue, the pro-nudging camp has not yet sufficiently addressed it. This article aims to fill this void by presenting a conditional defence of nudging when preferences are endogenous. We explain the learning process through which individuals establish ‘agentic’ preferences: preferences that are sufficiently stable, reasonable, autonomous and associated with organismic well-being to ground the ‘welfare’ principle of libertarian paternalism. To describe this process, we draw on theories from psychological science, in particular self-discrepancy theory and self-determination theory. We argue that agentic preferences are not only welfare-relevant and thus appropriate to libertarian paternalism but can also be identified by choice architects."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "70ca3a442c089f6cd060368021aacb9c32bf980814b757806b58f83792e2a57c", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "emergent_preferences", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.1007/978-3-662-48847-8_6", "doi:10.1109/roman.2007.4415149", "doi:10.1017/bpp.2022.17", "openalex:W2471937682", "openalex:W1970707217"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-48847-8_6", "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007%2F978-3-662-48847-8_6.pdf", "https://doi.org/10.1109/roman.2007.4415149", "https://uhra.herts.ac.uk/id/eprint/12780/1/902066.pdf", "https://doi.org/10.1017/bpp.2022.17", "https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/F304264D0BC894DAFA1A86EEFE50293E/S2398063X22000173a.pdf/div-class-title-agentic-preferences-a-foundation-for-nudging-when-preferences-are-endogenous-div.pdf"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}
